===Etymology 1===
{{etymon|en|:inh|enm:somer|tree=+|id=season}}
From {{inh|en|enm|somer}}, {{m|enm|sumer}}, from {{inh|en|ang|sumor|g=m}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*sumar|g=m}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*sumaraz|g=m}}, derived from {{der|en|ine-pro|*semh₂-|t=summer}}.<ref>{{R:gem:EDPG|head=''*sumara-'' n.|page=491f.}}</ref>

{{box|title=Cognates|
Cognate with {{cog|sco|somer}}, {{m|sco|sumer}}, {{m|sco|simer}}, {{cog|fy|simmer}}, {{cog|stq|Suumer}}, {{cog|nl|zomer}}, {{cog|nds|Sommer}}, {{cog|de|Sommer}},  {{cog|da,nb|sommer}}, {{cog|sv|sommar}}, {{cog|nn,is|sumar}}; {{cog|cy|haf}}, {{cog|hy|ամ|t=year}}, {{m|hy|ամառ|t=summer}}, {{cog|sa|समा|tr=sámā|t=a half-year, season, weather, year}}, {{cog|ae|𐬵𐬀𐬨|tr=ham-}}, {{cog|pal|ḥʾmyn|tr=hāmīn}}, {{cog|kmr|havîn}}, {{cog|ckb|ھاوین}}.
}}

====Alternative forms====
* {{alt|en|somer||obsolete or eye dialect}}
* {{alt|en|sommer||obsolete}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}
[[File:Senden, Dortmund-Ems-Kanal -- 2014 -- 2991-5.jpg|thumb|'''Summer''' in Germany]]

# {{senseid|en|Q1313}}One of four [[season]]s, traditionally the second, marked by the longest and typically hottest days of the year due to the inclination of the Earth and [[thermal]] [[lag]]. Typically regarded as spanning  either the period between the [[summer solstice]] to the [[autumnal equinox]], or the months of [[June]], [[July]], and [[August]] in the [[Northern Hemisphere]] and the months of [[December]], [[January]] and [[February]] in the [[Southern Hemisphere]].
#: {{ux|en|the heat of '''summer'''}}
#: {{ux|en|Gradually, the end of '''summer''' hove into sight.}} 
#: {{ux|en|I'm going to the Gambia in the '''summer'''.}} 
#* {{RQ:Besant Ivory Gate|II
|passage=At twilight in the '''summer''' there is never anybody to fear—man, woman, or cat—in the chambers and at that hour the mice come out. They do not eat parchment or foolscap or red tape, but they eat the luncheon crumbs.}}
#* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=1|passage=A chap named Eleazir Kendrick and I had chummed in together the '''summer''' afore and built a fish-weir and shanty at Setuckit Point, down Orham way. For a spell we done pretty well.}}
#* {{RQ:Allingham China Governess|chapter=5|passage=A waiter brought his aperitif, which was a small scotch and soda, and as he sipped it gratefully he sighed. &para; ‘Civilized,’ he said to Mr. Campion. ‘Humanizing.’{{...}}‘Cigars and '''summer''' days and women in big hats with swansdown face-powder, that's what it reminds me of.’}}
# {{lb|en|poetic|or|humorous}} [[year]]; {{ng|used to give the [[age]] of a person, usually a young one.}}
#: {{ux|en|He was barely eighteen '''summers''' old.}}
#: {{ux|en|She had seen not more than twenty '''summers'''.}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} Most [[flourish]]ing, [[happy]], or [[beautiful]] [[period]]; [[golden age]], [[prime]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1829|author=w:Edgar Allan Poe|chapter={{w|Tamerlane (poem)|Tamerlane}}|title=Al Aaraaf, Tamerlane and Minor Poems|passage=O! craving heart, for the lost flowers<br>And sunshine of my '''summer''' hours!}}
#* {{quote-song|en|author={{w|Billy Bragg}}|title={{w|Greetings to the New Brunette}}|album={{w|Talking with the Taxman About Poetry}}|year=1986|note=track 1|passage=Here we are in our '''summer''' years / Livin' on ice cream and chocolate kisses / Would the leaves fall from the trees / If I was your old man and you was my missus}}
# {{lb|en|countable|fashion}} Someone with light, pinkish skin that has a blue undertone, light hair and eyes, seen as best suited to certain colors of clothing.

