{{en-noun}}
[[Image:Strait.svg|thumb|right|150 px|Sketch of a strait connecting two larger bodies of water.]]

# {{senseid|en|Q37901}}{{lb|en|geography}} A narrow [[channel]] of water connecting two larger [[body of water|bodies of water]].
#: {{ux|en|the '''Strait''' of Gibraltar}}
#* {{RQ:Defoe Captain Singleton|pages=232–233|pageref=233|passage={{...}} we steered directly through a large Out-let, which they call a '''Streight''', tho’ it be fifteen Miles broad {{...}}}}
# A narrow pass, passage or street.
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=2|canto=7|page=282|passage=He brought him through a darksom narrow  '''strayt''', <br> To a broad gate all built of beaten gold:}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Troilus and Cressida|act=III|scene=iii|passage=For honour travels in a '''strait''' so narrow, <br> Where one but goes abreast:}}
# A [[neck]] of land; an [[isthmus]].
#* {{RQ:Tennyson Poems 1842|title=Morte d’Arthur|volume=II|page=4|passage=The bold Sir Bedivere uplifted him, <br> Sir Bedivere, the last of all his knights, <br> And bore him to a chapel nigh the field, <br> A broken chancel with a broken cross, <br> That stood on a dark '''strait''' of barren land.}}
# {{lb|en|often|in the plural}} A difficult position.
#: {{ux|en|to be in [[dire straits|dire '''straits''']]}}
#* '''1684''', {{w|Robert South}}, “A Sermon Preached at Westminster-Abbey” in ''Twelve Sermons Preached upon Several Occasions'', London: Thomas Bennett, 1692, p. 420,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A60954.0001.001]</sup>
#*: {{...}} let no man, who owns the Belief of a Providence, grow desperate or forlorn, under any Calamity or '''Strait''' whatsoever {{...}}
#* {{RQ:Homer Pope et al Odyssey|volume=III|page=19|passage=Plutarch is of opinion that this sleep of Ulysses was feigned; and that he made use of the pretence of natural infirmity to conceal the '''streights''' he was in at that time in his thoughts {{...}}}}

====Usage notes====
* The geographical term ''straits'' used in the name of a location (as a [[proper noun]]) does not necessarily imply the existence of multiple straits; see for instance the [[Bering Straits]], the [[Straits of Gibraltar]], the [[Straits of Hormuz]], the [[Straits of Juan de Fuca]], the [[Straits of Magellan]], the [[Straits of Malacca]], the [[Sunda Straits]], the [[Taiwan Straits]], etc.

