===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|stoute}}, from {{der|en|fro|estout|t=brave, fierce, proud}} (Modern {{cog|fr|-}} dialectal {{m|fr|stout|t=proud}}), from earlier {{der|en|fro|estolt|t=strong}}, from {{der|en|frk|*stolt}}, {{m|frk|*stult|t=bold, proud}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*stultaz|t=bold, proud}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*stel-|t=to put, stand}}.

Cognate with {{cog|nl|stout||stout, bold, naughty}}, {{cog|nds|stolt||stately, proud}}, {{cog|de|stolz||proud, haughty, arrogant, stately}}, {{cog|non|stoltr||proud}} ({{cog|da|stolt||proud}}, {{cog|is|stoltur||proud}}).

Meaning "strong in body, powerfully built" was {{etydate|c|1386|nocap=1}}, but has been to a large extent displaced by the euphemistic meaning "thick-bodied, fat and large," which is first recorded 1804. Original sense preserved in stout-hearted (1552).

The noun "strong, dark-brown beer" is first recorded 1677, from the adjective.

