==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|sturdy}}, {{m|enm|stourdy}}, {{m|enm|stordy|t=bold, valiant, strong, stern, fierce, rebellious}} (perhaps influenced by {{noncog|enm|sture}}, {{m|enm|stoure}}, {{m|enm|stor|t=strong, robust, harsh, stern, violent, fierce, sturdy}}; see {{noncog|en|stour}}), from {{der|en|fro|estourdi|t=dazed}}, form of {{m|fro|estourdir}}, originally “to daze, to make [[tipsy]] (almost drunk)” (Modern French {{m|fr|étourdir||to daze, to make tipsy}}), from {{der|en|la-vul|*exturdire}}. {{uder|en|la|-}} etymology is unclear – presumably it is {{m|la|ex-}} + {{m|la|turdus||[[thrush]] (bird)}}, but how this should mean “daze” is unclear.<ref name="oetyld">{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary}}</ref> A speculative theory is that thrushes eat leftover winery grapes and thus became drunk, but this meets with objections.<ref>OED</ref>

Disease in cows and sheep is by extension of sense of “daze”, while sense of “strongly built” is of late 14th century,<ref name="oetyld" /> and relationship to earlier sense is less clear, perhaps from sense of a firm strike (causing a daze) or a strong, violent person.

