===Etymology 1===
{{PIE word|en|upó}}
The {{glossary|noun}} is derived from Late {{inh|en|enm|somet}}, {{m|enm|somete|t=head, top}}{{nb...|somette, sommet, sommete, sommette|otherforms=1}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=somet|pos=n|id=MED41489}}</ref> from {{der|en|xno|sumet}} and {{der|en|frm|sommet}} {{qualifier|{{glossary|masculine}}}}, {{m|frm|somete}}, {{m|frm|sommette|t=top of a thing; highest point of a mountain}} {{qualifier|{{glossary|feminine}}}} (modern {{cog|fr|sommet}}), from {{der|en|fro|somet}}, {{m|fro|sommette}}, from {{m|fro|som}}, {{m|fro|sum|t=highest point, summit}} + {{m|fro|-et|pos={{glossary|suffix}} forming {{glossary|diminutive}} masculine nouns}}, {{m|fro|-ete}}, {{m|fro|-ette|pos=suffix forming diminutive feminine nouns}}. {{m|fro||Som}}, {{m|fro||sum}} are derived from {{der|en|la|summum|t=top, summit}}, a noun use of the {{glossary|neuter}} of {{m|la|summus|t=greatest, highest; top, uppermost|pos=adjective}}<ref>{{R:Lexico|pos=n}}</ref> (ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*upér|t=over}} + {{m|ine-pro|*-tm̥mos|*-m̥mos, *-tm̥mos|suffix forming {{glossary|superlative}} adjectives}}). The modern English spelling was influenced by {{m|en|summity|t=height or top of a thing; utmost degree, perfection}} {{qualifier|obsolete}}.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=n|id=5661760872|date=September 2023}}</ref>

The {{glossary|verb}} is derived from the noun.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=''v.''{{sup|2}}|noformat=1|id=2122364155|date=July 2023|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=v}}</ref>

