# {{lb|en|transitive|climbing|informal}} To [[reach#Verb|reach]] the [[summit#Noun|summit]] ''(noun {{senseno|en|peak}})'' of (a [[mountain]]).
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Kenza Moller|title=Eyes on the North|journal=w:Canadian Geographic|location=Ottawa|publisher=w:Royal Canadian Geographical Society|month=July–August|year=2012|volume=132|issue=4|page=10|issn=0706-2168|oclc=906279700|passage=Of the range's 12 peaks, Mount Saskatchewan is the only one that has yet to be '''summited'''.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|climbing|informal}} To reach the summit of a mountain.
# {{lb|en|intransitive|politics}} To [[attend]] a summit ''(noun {{senseno|en|gathering}})''.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1969|journal=Economic and Political Weekly|volume=4|issue=14-26|page=645
|passage=If the Soviet leaders could go on '''summiting''' with the US while bombs poured on North Vietnam and yet claim that they had nothing but the best interests of the Vietnamese revolution in mind, there seems precious little reason to cry wolf at Peking-Bonn relations.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1972|journal=Newsweek|volume=79|page=51
|passage={{...}} what the North Vietnamese would do while Richard Nixon was '''summiting''' in Moscow. {{...|Based on the enemy's well-developed flair for drama, there was a widespread fear that the worst was about to happen.}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1998|author=David S. Nuttall|title=Mickey Mantle's Greatest Hits|page=152
|passage=The young President [JFK], who is on the eve of '''summiting''' with Soviet Premier Nikita Khrushchev, is ensconced at Hyannis Port, Massachusetts, preparing to fly to Vienna with his wife, Mrs. Jacqueline Kennedy, to meet with Khrushchev to discuss Cold War issues.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2001|author=Robert V. Heffernan|title=Cabinetmakers: Story of the Three-year Battle to Establish the U.S. Department of Education|page=439
|passage=The President had been '''summiting''' with Soviet President Leonid Brezhnev in Vienna.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2021|author=Mark LaVoie|title=Reagan’s Soviet Rhetoric: Telling the Soviet Redemption Story|page=92
|passage=From May 29 to June 3, 1988[,] Reagan and Gorbachev '''summited''' in Moscow. Expectations of the meeting were low, because Reagan's position as a "lame duck" president guaranteed that nothing new of historic import would occur.}}

